WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
Tomorrow is packed with meetings.

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:06.820
Where do coffee, lunch, and a good night's sleep fit?

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:11.160
Dayweave connects Gmail, Google Calendar, and Google Sheets to help you make

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.460
room. Here's a walkthrough with sample data.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:18.800
It starts with your usual coffee time, using timestamped purchases when there's

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.720
enough history. That's a useful clue, though a receipt can't tell us

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.480
what you actually drank.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.460
Then it works backward from your first commitment, your morning routine, and

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:32.380
your chosen sleep target, so you can plan tonight with tomorrow in

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:33.400
mind.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.900
When meetings crowd out lunch, it finds a full break that really

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.300
fits, and explains why that time works.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:45.760
Gemini brings these signals together, using read-only tools to choose a focus.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.980
Scheduling rules check the times, and you can follow the agent's reasoning

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.080
in its activity trace.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:55.840
You're still in control. Choose a suggestion, review the exact calendar event

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.600
and Sheets update, then confirm.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.780
The event appears in your schedule.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:05.000
These sample changes are simulated, and the app labels them clearly.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.120
Every action gets a record.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.740
Repeat the same approval, and it avoids creating duplicates.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.640
And when history is thin, it says so, using your preferences instead

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:15.520
of guessing at a habit.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:20.840
We also tested a real Google account: reading Gmail and Calendar, saving

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.740
preferences in Sheets, and creating an approved lunch event with its activity

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:28.720
log. Repeating that action created no duplicate.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.400
Next, we'd connect a wearable, with the user's authorization, to bring in

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.720
sleep and activity data. That's planned, not connected today.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.080
Dayweave makes tomorrow easier to act on, one choice at a time.
